for more on John McCain we're joined by  CBS this morning co-host John Dickerson  John good morning good morning Anthony  John you you spent a lot of time  covering John McCain over the years and  and to what degree is the service that's  gonna be happening this morning a  reflection of what John McCain was all  about well he authored it in in a sense  I mean he asked former President Bush  and Obama to speak at his service and it  tells you two things just those those  two voices one these are people against  whom he ran bitter campaigns and having  covered both of them and heard the  senator himself talk about his opponents  it is not that he said some things that  you cannot repeat in polite company and  felt very and and he was a competitor  and and so let's not mistake the way he  fought those campaigns when they were  done however he tried his best to work  with those two presidents and he became  close to both of them in in very  specific ways so that's part of his  legacy is and anybody who tried to  normalize relations with Vietnam the  country that had held him for five and a  half years that's in keeping with that  but also it's a symbol and it's a it's  very much in keeping with the famous  speech he gave at the end of his career  on the Senate floor talking about  regular order and regular order is  Democrats and Republicans fighting like  heck and then in the end trying to do  the best thing for the country and so  the symbolism of this service is is  meant to match that message and to that  point John he called himself an  imperfect public servant and his ability  to change his mind really struck me I  want to go to that point because I  remember the MLK holiday vote and I  remember that he was against it and yet  he turned around during the 2008  campaign saying we can be slowed as well  to give greatness it's do a mistake I  made I was wrong and eventually realized  it and and and and I'd remind you that  we can all be a little late sometimes in  doing the right thing and dr. King  understood this about his fellow  Americans what did you take about his  ability to change on subjects well it  reminds me a little bit of  Churchill quote which I'll paraphrase  about America which is America always  does the right thing it just takes them  a while to get around to doing it and in  this case McCain and this was true of  the Confederate flag too in South  Carolina he said it was a symbol of  heritage during the the primary there to  keep himself viable within the  Republican ranks after he lost the  primary and the and the run for the  presidency in 2000 he went back down and  said I was wrong and this is something  we don't see much in politics which is  politicians saying they're wrong but  also this was a part of trying to  maintain a standard he believed in  standards of personal conduct and also  institutional conduct that you you tried  to keep the standard and when you fell  you acknowledged it and then tried to  hold that standard up again as opposed  to the political thing which is usually  to do whatever you please and then  redefine the standard so that so that  you don't have to explain yourself he  was very hard on himself as a politician  in public and in private and that's  certainly part of his legacy John you  had an up-close look at him you had a  chance to cover him on the straight talk  express and you had pretty unfettered  access to him from what I understand I  wonder if that exists today or if  there's a politician that you can think  of that as as as accessible as John  McCain was to reporters it doesn't exist  today and it really didn't exist for  McCain in 2008 that was one of the  problems and challenges of his campaign  when you talk to the people who served  with him and his fellow POWs they said  after the war McCain was starving to  learn as much as he could about the war  and just make up for lost time he  retained that energy and that thrust for  his entire life and that's what covering  him on the campaign was like it was one  long rolling conversation and it was all  over the map from literature to film to  stories and and that energy is really at  the heart of my recollections of that  time period and then also on this somber  day it is very important to remember his  wit in the in his five and a half years  in captivity he was the storyteller  they didn't have any other entertainment  and so he would tell stories as we  talked to John fer one of his cellmates  who you know in Christmas he played  Scrooge in  a rendition of a Christmas carol that  storytelling was at the heart of both  his political success in the campaign  and also what he used to do back there  at the back of the bus all right John  Dickerson in Washington with this more  Washington this morning for us thanks so  much John for being with us Joe think  and stay with CBS News throughout the  morning in just about 20 minutes this  show will be abbreviated and we will go  to Washington for a special report on  Senator McCain's memorial service at the  National Cathedral
 

